    {"exercise": "188", "solution": "The five terms are Westview, Dawn, Zenith, Eastside, Harvest. First: Westview (term 1). Last: Harvest (term 5). Immediately after Westview: Dawn (term 2). Immediately before Eastside: Zenith (term 3).", "gradingConsideration": "Named-team composite responses need free review under the no-exact constraint; do not substitute numerical ranks for the requested first/last team names."},
    {"exercise": "189", "solution": "Six terms, not seven: the Period/Subject heading is not a term. Terms 3 and 4 are both Literature. Sequence: Chemistry, Algebra, Literature, Literature, Drafting, PE.", "gradingConsideration": "The count is numerical, but the full question also requests identification of repeated terms; a complete composite response may be ungraded."},
    {"exercise": "190", "solution": "Examples: PE, English, Mathematics, Nature; English, Mathematics, Nature, PE; Mathematics, PE, English, Nature. Each includes each required subject once. There are 4! = 24 possible schedules, but enumerating all 24 is not required.", "gradingConsideration": "Open construction; ungraded."},
    {"exercise": "191", "solution": "a) X={1,2,3,4,5}, Y={1,3,5,7,9}; arrows 1->1,2->3,3->5,4->7,5->9; ordered pairs (1,1),(2,3),(3,5),(4,7),(5,9). b) X={1,2,3,4,5,6}, Y={10,20,30}; arrows 1->10,2->20,3->30,4->10,5->20,6->30; pairs (1,10),(2,20),(3,30),(4,10),(5,20),(6,30). c) X={1,2,3,4,5,6}, Y={-1,1}; arrows 1->1,2->-1,3->1,4->-1,5->1,6->-1; pairs (1,1),(2,-1),(3,1),(4,-1),(5,1),(6,-1). Y lists distinct values, not six repeated values.", "gradingConsideration": "Sets plus two representations of each mapping are a composite response; ungraded is appropriate."},
    {"exercise": "192", "solution": "a) The two-digit multiples of 13 are 13,26,39,52,65,78,91: b_1=13,b_4=52,b_7=91. b) z_1=-1,z_4=0,z_100=0,z_253=-1,z_(2k)=0,z_(2k+1)=-1 for positive integer k.", "gradingConsideration": "Nine independently labeled numeric parts; reliably auto-gradable."},
    {"exercise": "193", "solution": "a) After a_1: a_2; after a_4: a_5. b) Before a_2: a_1; before a_5: a_4. c) a_1 has no predecessor. d) a_6 has no successor.", "gradingConsideration": "Six indexed term symbols, not their unknown values or bare indices. Assess candidate's grading choice without inventing a value for a_i."},
    {"exercise": "194", "solution": "a) c_11,c_(k+1),c_(k+4),c_(k-1),c_(2k+1). b) c_49,c_(k-1),c_(k+6),c_(k-5),c_(2k+2). All referenced indices must be positive integers. In a), c_(k-2) requires k>=3. In b), the predecessor of c_k requires k>=2 and the predecessor of c_(k-4) requires k>=6; at k=5 the latter is c_1 with no predecessor.", "gradingConsideration": "Indexed symbols with existence conditions form a composite answer; do not mistake subscripts for values."},
    {"exercise": "195", "solution": "a) b_21,b_22,b_23,b_24. b) b_(k+1),b_(k+2),b_(k+3),b_(k+4),b_(k+5),b_(k+6). c) b_(k-1),b_k,b_(k+1),b_(k+2), with k>=3 so the stated endpoint b_(k-2) exists. Endpoints themselves are excluded.", "gradingConsideration": "Lists of indexed term symbols; composite ungraded response is permissible."},
    {"exercise": "196", "solution": "a) n. b) n-1 (n>=2). c) n-4 (n>=5). d) n-k+1 for positive integers 1<=k<=n.", "gradingConsideration": "Four scalar algebraic expressions are reliably auto-gradable with expression judges."},
    {"exercise": "197", "solution": "Increasing: (b_n),(d_n). Decreasing: (a_n),(c_n). (x_n) is neither due to alternating rises/falls. (y_n) is neither: adjacent equal values exclude strict increase, and it has rises so is not decreasing.", "gradingConsideration": "Classification lists with explanation; ungraded under no-exact is permissible."},
    {"exercise": "198", "solution": "Examples a) 1,2,3,4,... (strict increase); b) -1,-2,-3,-4,... (strict decrease); c) 1,-1,1,-1,... (neither). Other correct examples, including a constant sequence for c), are valid.", "gradingConsideration": "Open examples; ungraded."},
    {"exercise": "199", "solution": "a) 7/12=0.583333... with only the 3 repeating. x_1=0.6,x_2=0.58,x_3=0.583,x_10=0.5833333333. No two terms are equal: after x_2 each extra place adds a positive 3, and x_1 is larger than all later terms. Neither increasing nor decreasing since x_1>x_2<x_3. b) 23/45=0.511111... with the 1 repeating. 0.5111111111 is y_10; y_1=0.5,y_2=0.51,y_3=0.511,... is strictly increasing.", "gradingConsideration": "Decimal conversion, specified terms, repetition and classification together form a composite response; ungraded is permissible without claiming only numeric subanswers complete the task."},
    {"exercise": "200", "solution": "a) Plot exactly (1,5),(2,3),(3,1),(4,-1),(5,-3),(6,-5). b) Plot exactly (1,-1),(2,3),(3,0),(4,-2),(5,4). Horizontal coordinate is term index; vertical coordinate is term value. No connecting lines.", "gradingConsideration": "Graph construction; ungraded, but display must give all points and key plotting instructions."},
    {"exercise": "201", "solution": "a_n values -3,-1,0,2,2.5,5 are strictly increasing. b_n values -4.5,-8,-10.5,-12,-12.5,-12,-10.5,-8,-4.5 fall then rise; neither. c_n values 7,6,3,2,1,0.5,4 fall then rise; neither. d_n values 5,4.5,-2.5,-0.5,-1,-1.5 include a rise from term 3 to 4; neither. Only (a_n) is increasing. No decreasing sequence.", "gradingConsideration": "Classification plus graphical explanation; ungraded is permissible. Do not classify c_n or d_n from only their initial decline."},
    {"exercise": "202", "solution": "Let positive side lengths be u,v cm. u+v=20. a) uv=84 gives t^2-20t+84=(t-6)(t-14), so sides 6 cm and 14 cm; exists. b) uv=105 impossible because uv<=((u+v)/2)^2=100 cm^2 (equivalently discriminant 400-420=-20<0); no such rectangle.", "gradingConsideration": "Existence plus nonexistence justification is composite/proof-like; ungraded is permissible. Side labels cm, area cm^2."},
    {"exercise": "203", "solution": "a) y=5-x gives x^2+5=30, x=+-5; points (-5,10) and (5,0). b) y=2x gives x^2+8x-20=(x-2)(x+10)=0; points (-10,-20) and (2,4). Both points in each subpart are required; coordinate pairs themselves are ordered but the two-point solution set is unordered.", "gradingConsideration": "Coordinates can be graded reliably by separately labeled numeric x/y parts with an explicit root-order convention; do not accept cross-paired roots."}
